or NFIP.</p><ul class="c3-fi-three" role="list" aria-label="What happens during a lapse of federal flood insurance"><li><span class="hd"><span class="c3-fi-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span>No new policies</span><p>FEMA cannot sell a new federal flood policy until Congress acts.</p></li><li><span class="hd"><span class="c3-fi-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span>Current policies stay in force</span><p>A policy you already have covers you to the end of its one-year term.</p></li><li><span class="hd"><span class="c3-fi-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>Claims are still paid</span><p>FEMA pays claims from the premiums it collects. Its borrowing limit drops from about $30 billion to $1 billion.</p></li></ul><figure class="c3-fi-time" role="group" aria-label="Timeline: federal flood insurance expires Dec. 11, before the new Senate term starts Jan. 3"><span class="cap">The flood insurance deadline comes before the new senator</span><ol><li><b>Sept. 2</b><span>The stopgap spending law is signed. It keeps federal flood insurance running to Dec. 11.</span></li><li><b>Nov. 3</b><span>Election Day. The Associated Press calls the Senate race for Annie Andrews.</span></li><li class="key"><b>Dec. 11</b><span>Federal flood insurance expires unless Congress extends it. The Congress in office now votes.</span></li><li><b>Jan. 3</b><span>The new Senate term starts. Sen. Darline Graham's term ends.</span></li><li><b>After Jan. 3</b><span>Andrews votes on any later extension or long-term flood bill.</span></li></ol></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who votes on the Dec. 11 deadline?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Congress in office now. Sen. Darline Graham, <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/13/trump-lindsey-graham-darline-south-carolina-senator.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">appointed in July</a> after her brother, Sen. Lindsey Graham, died, holds the seat until her term ends on Jan. 3. Russell Fry, who represents Horry County in the U.S. House, won re-election and votes before and after January.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our guide to <a href="/market-reports/2026-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what the election means for Myrtle Beach buyers and sellers</a> covers every race and what each office controls.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="past-lapses"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Past lapses and closings">Has flood insurance lapsed before, and did closings stop?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Congress has let federal flood insurance lapse nine times since 2010. Each lapse ended when Congress passed an extension.</p><figure style="margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:760px"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 494" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of the nine lapses of federal flood insurance since 2010: March 1, 2010, 1 day; March 29, 2010, about 2½ weeks; June 1, 2010, about a month; Oct. 1, 2011, 4 days; Jan. 20, 2018, 2 days; Feb. 9, 2018, 8 hours; March 23, 2024, about 13 hours; Oct. 1, 2025, about 6 weeks; Feb. 1, 2026, 2 days." style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:400px"><text x="0" y="23" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">March 1, 2010</text><rect x="0" y="34" width="5" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="13" y="47" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">1 day</text><text x="0" y="77" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">March 29, 2010</text><rect x="0" y="88" width="85" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="93" y="101" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">about 2½ weeks</text><text x="0" y="131" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">June 1, 2010</text><rect x="0" y="142" width="155" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="163" y="155" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">about a month</text><text x="0" y="185" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">Oct. 1, 2011</text><rect x="0" y="196" width="20" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="28" y="209" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">4 days</text><text x="0" y="239" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">Jan. 20, 2018</text><rect x="0" y="250" width="10" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="18" y="263" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">2 days</text><text x="0" y="293" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">Feb. 9, 2018</text><rect x="0" y="304" width="4" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="12" y="317" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">8 hours</text><text x="0" y="347" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">March 23, 2024</text><rect x="0" y="358" width="4" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="12" y="371" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">about 13 hours</text><text x="0" y="401" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">Oct. 1, 2025</text><rect x="0" y="412" width="210" height="15" rx="7" fill="#a8612c"/><text x="218" y="425" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">about 6 weeks</text><text x="0" y="455" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">Feb. 1, 2026</text><rect x="0" y="466" width="10" height="15" rx="7" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="18" y="479" font-family="Inter,system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16" fill="#3d4450">2 days</text></svg><figcaption style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:.6rem">How long each lapse of federal flood insurance lasted, by the day it started. Chart: Chapter3 Realty, from the Congressional Research Service's record of each lapse.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What did a lapse do to home closings?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the month-long lapse of June 2010, an estimated 1,400 home closings a day were canceled or delayed across the country. During the six-week lapse of October and November 2025, some lenders suspended the flood insurance requirement for buyers.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A lapse matters most to a buyer with a loan on a home in a high-risk flood zone. The lender must require flood insurance there before closing. Most buyers meet that rule with a federal policy.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="horry"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Horry County's flood policies">How many Horry County homes have federal flood insurance?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1 in 7 residential buildings in Horry County has a federal flood policy, 20,388 policies in all. Of those, 5,861 are on homes in the high-risk flood zone, where a lender requires one. A home outside that zone can still flood, and our agents check a house's flood zone and <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">flood history</a> before you offer.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Share of homes with a federal flood policy, three coastal counties"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Share of homes with a federal flood policy, three coastal counties</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">County, policies</span><span role="columnheader">Share of homes</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Charleston County</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">46,856 policies</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.792" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31.7%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Georgetown County</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">5,655 policies</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.516" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20.7%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Horry County</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">20,388 policies</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.366" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">14.6%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Federal flood policies on homes, as a share of all residential buildings in each county. FEMA counts policies, and one policy can cover a whole condo building.</figcaption></figure><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/florence-rain-carolinas-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/florence-rain-carolinas-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/florence-rain-carolinas-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="840" alt="NASA satellite map of rain from Hurricane Florence over North and South Carolina, heaviest in red over southeastern North Carolina, with the storm's track shown as a dotted line" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-NASA" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Map: NASA. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Map: NASA</a></span></div><figcaption>Rain from Hurricane Florence, Sept. 13 to 16, 2018, estimated by NASA satellites. Red marks the most rain. Horry County is the South Carolina coast just south of the state line. The dotted line is the storm's track.</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="yearly-limits"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Yearly increase limits">How much can flood insurance go up each year in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Under federal law, most flood premiums can rise no more than 18% a year. A <a href="/buyers/second-home/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">second home</a> still on an older discounted rate is different. Its premium must rise 25% a year until it reaches the full-risk price.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Years</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Most homes</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Older discounted second home</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">1</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+18%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+25%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">2</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+39%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+56%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">3</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+64%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+95%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">5</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+129%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">+205%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At the lower limit, a premium can grow to more than twice its starting amount in five years. At the higher one, it can reach three times.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">These limits stay the same whoever holds the Senate seat. Only a new law from Congress changes them. Our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to coastal insurance in Myrtle Beach</a> gives what flood, wind and homeowners policies cost here.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="one-senator"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What one senator can do">What can one U.S. senator change about flood insurance?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A U.S. senator can vote, sponsor bills and serve on committees. One senator cannot change flood insurance alone.</p><ul class="c3-fi-tiles" role="list" aria-label="What one senator's vote means"><li><b>1 of 100</b><span>is Andrews' share of the Senate's votes.</span></li><li><b>60 votes</b><span>are needed to <a href="https://www.senate.gov/about/powers-procedures/filibusters-cloture.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">end debate</a> on a regular bill in the Senate.</span></li><li><b>Banking</b><span>is the Senate committee that gets FEMA's flood insurance cost recommendations.</span></li></ul><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/us-capitol-west-front-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="623" alt="The west front of the United States Capitol in Washington, with the dome in the middle, the Senate wing on the left and the House wing on the right" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Congress-AOC" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Architect of the Capitol. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: Architect of the Capitol</a></span></div><figcaption>The United States Capitol from the west. The Senate meets in the wing on the left, and the House in the wing on the right.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Federal flood law sends FEMA's recommendations on flood insurance costs to the <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/4015" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Senate Committee on Banking, Housing, and Urban Affairs</a>. Committee seats for the new Congress are not assigned yet, so Andrews' committees are not known.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A long-term flood bill needs those Senate votes, a majority in the House and the president's signature. Her <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">first-time buyer tax credit idea</a> needs the same votes, unless it goes in a budget bill, which needs only a simple majority in the Senate.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">CBS News projects that Democrats will control the Senate in January. Before the election, Republicans held 53 seats to 47.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-they-said"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What the winners said">What have Andrews and Wilson said about insurance?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Neither winner has a written flood insurance plan that we found. Andrews held a roundtable on homeowners insurance. Wilson's agenda, as reported, calls for state audits of rate increases.</p><div class="c3-fi-two"><section aria-label="Annie Andrews on insurance"><h3>Annie Andrews</h3><p class="who">Democrat. Won the U.S. Senate race; the Associated Press called it on election night.</p><dl><dt>What she said about insurance</dt><dd>She held a <a href="https://www.wtoc.com/2026/10/14/democratic-senate-nominee-holds-insurance-roundtable-beaufort/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">homeowners insurance roundtable in Beaufort</a> with Sen. Sheldon Whitehouse. Her campaign said homeowners insurance in Beaufort and Jasper counties is projected to rise more than 200% in 10 years. The campaign did not say where that projection came from.</dd><dt>A written flood insurance plan?</dt><dd>None that we found. Her <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/issue/transportation-infrastructure-that-works/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">infrastructure page</a> backs "resilient infrastructure" and names no flood project.</dd><dt>Can her office change federal flood insurance?</dt><dd>Only with Congress. She has one Senate vote and, once assigned, committee seats.</dd><dt>Does it change the Dec. 11 deadline?</dt><dd>No. She takes office after it.</dd></dl></section><section aria-label="Alan Wilson on insurance"><h3>Alan Wilson</h3><p class="who">Republican. Won the governor's race; Jermaine Johnson conceded on election night.</p><dl><dt>What he said about insurance</dt><dd>His <a href="https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/10/15/republican-nominee-sc-governor-alan-wilson-announces-affordability-agenda/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">affordability agenda</a>, as reported, calls for regular, public audits of rate increases. The state Department of Insurance would use the audit power it already has. The governor would get quarterly updates, and major carriers would publish yearly reports.</dd><dt>A written flood insurance plan?</dt><dd>No flood item is in his agenda as reported. The audit item names rate increases and does not mention flood insurance. His agenda also calls for <a href="/invest/rental-property-tax-cut/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">removing the higher property tax on rentals</a>.</dd><dt>Can his office change federal flood insurance?</dt><dd>No. Congress writes the law for federal flood insurance.</dd><dt>Does it change the Dec. 11 deadline?</dt><dd>No. A governor has no vote in Congress.</dd></dl></section></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For the rest of each winner's plans, see <a href="/market-reports/2026-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what the 2026 election means for Myrtle Beach</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="private"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Private flood insurance">Can I use private flood insurance instead of FEMA in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. A private flood policy can meet a lender's flood insurance rule when it meets the federal standards for one. Most loans still use a federal policy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A lapse does not stop the sale of private flood policies. Our agents have a network of insurance agents for federal and private flood quotes.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Closing on a home in a flood zone?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We help get a federal and a private flood insurance quote on the home, so you can compare them before Dec. 11. There is no cost to you.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Talk to a specialized agent</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="next"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Short and long run">What will happen to flood insurance on the Grand Strand next?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The past record points to another short extension or a short lapse in the next year or two. Over five years and more, it points to more short extensions.</p><ul class="c3-fi-runs" role="list" aria-label="What the past record points to, in the short and long run"><li><span class="hd"><span class="c3-fi-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span>Short run: the next year or two</span><p><b>What happened before</b>A short extension at most deadlines since late 2017, and five short lapses. Every lapse so far ended with an extension.</p><p><b>What it points to here</b>Another short extension near Dec. 11, or a lapse of hours to weeks, as before.</p><p><b>What has to happen first</b>Both chambers of the Congress in office now pass an extension, and the president signs it.</p></li><li><span class="hd"><span class="c3-fi-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>Premiums, every year</span><p><b>What the law says now</b>A premium can rise each year up to the limits in the table above.</p><p><b>What it points to here</b>The same limits, with or without a new senator.</p><p><b>What has to happen first</b>Only a new law from Congress changes the limits.</p></li><li><span class="hd"><span class="c3-fi-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 8.5h10.5a3 3 0 1 0-3-3M3 12.5h15a3 3 0 1 1-3 3M3 16.5h6"/></svg></span>Long run: five years and more</span><p><b>What happened before</b>Nine years of short extensions, with no long-term renewal.</p><p><b>What it points to here</b>More short extensions, unless a long-term bill passes. We found no study of what the short extensions did to home prices.</p><p><b>What has to happen first</b>A bill through the Senate Banking Committee and 60 Senate votes to end debate. Then a House majority and the president's signature.</p></li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a lapse look like for one buyer?</h3><div class="c3-story" style="background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);padding:1.5rem 1.5rem .7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.4rem;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 .9rem"><strong>Example:</strong> A buyer is under contract on a house near the Waccamaw River, in a high-risk flood zone. The closing is in mid-December, after the flood insurance deadline.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 .9rem">Her lender requires flood insurance to close. Congress has not voted yet.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:640px;margin:0 0 .9rem">A Chapter3 agent asks an insurance agent for a federal quote and a private quote weeks before the deadline. The lender accepts the private policy, and the closing stays on its date.</p></div><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:500;font-size:var(--read,1rem);line-height:1.6;max-width:34em;margin-bottom:1rem">Asking for both quotes early gives a buyer a second choice if Congress misses the deadline.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="flood-insurance-and-the-election-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Flood insurance and the election: questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the latest NFIP reauthorization update?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Federal flood insurance runs until Dec. 11 under the stopgap spending law signed on Sept. 2. Congress must extend it by then, or FEMA cannot sell new flood policies until it does.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What happens to my flood insurance if the program lapses on Dec. 11?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Your current policy stays in force to the end of its one-year term, and FEMA keeps paying claims. FEMA cannot sell new policies until Congress acts. A buyer who needs a new policy waits or uses private flood insurance.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Will Annie Andrews vote on the Dec. 11 flood insurance deadline?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. The new Senate term starts Jan. 3, after the deadline. Sen. Darline Graham holds the seat until then. Andrews votes on any extension or flood bill after she takes office.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a home closing happen during a flood insurance lapse?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, with a private flood policy the lender accepts. Some lenders also waived the requirement in the lapse that ran from October to November 2025. In the June 2010 lapse, an estimated 1,400 closings a day were canceled or delayed nationwide.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can the governor of South Carolina change flood insurance?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Only Congress can change federal flood insurance. Alan Wilson's insurance item, as reported, is about state audits of rate increases, with yearly public reports from major carriers.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IN/HTML/IN10835.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CRS: NFIP reauthorization</a>, <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-119publ103/html/PLAW-119publ103.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public Law 119-103</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/4015" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">42 U.S.C. 4015</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/4014" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">42 U.S.C. 4014</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/api/open/v1/NfipResidentialPenetrationRates?$filter=fipsCode%20eq%20'45051'" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA: Horry County flood policies</a>, <a href="https://www.senate.gov/about/powers-procedures/filibusters-cloture.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Senate: cloture</a>, <a href="https://www.wbtv.com/2026/11/04/democrat-annie-andrews-wins-us-senate-seat-south-carolina-ap-projects/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WBTV: AP call</a>, <a href="https://www.wtoc.com/2026/10/14/democratic-senate-nominee-holds-insurance-roundtable-beaufort/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WTOC: Andrews roundtable</a>, <a href="https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/10/15/republican-nominee-sc-governor-alan-wilson-announces-affordability-agenda/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fox Carolina: Wilson agenda</a>. Lapse lengths and premium growth: Chapter3's arithmetic from these sources. Election results are unofficial until certified.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">The United States Capitol from the west. Photo: Architect of the Capitol, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Congress-AOC" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Capitol_-_west_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized this photo.</div><div role="listitem">NASA satellite map of rain from Hurricane Florence over North and South Carolina, heaviest in red over southeastern North Carolina, with the storm's track shown as a dotted line. Photo: NASA Earth Observatory (Joshua Stevens and Lauren Dauphin), <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-NASA" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/images/92775/florence-inundates-the-carolinas" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA Earth Observatory</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped the open ocean on the right and resized this map.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-e/flood-insurance-after-the-election/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":623,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"The United States Capitol from the west","caption":"The United States Capitol from the west. The Senate meets in the wing on the left, and the House in the wing on the right.","description":"The west front of the United States Capitol in Washington, with the dome in the middle, the Senate wing on the left and the House wing on the right","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Architect of the Capitol"},"creditText":"Architect of the Capitol, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (Architect of the Capitol)","license":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Congress-AOC","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Capitol_-_west_front.jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Capitol_-_west_front.jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"United States Capitol, west front, Washington, D.C., South Carolina"},"representativeOfPa