oration:none">weather page</a> shows month by month, and the <a href="/buyers/relocating/which-town/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">town by town page</a> compares each town.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The income tax</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Five percent flat sounds close to ours. It mostly is.</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Massachusetts charges 5 percent on nearly everything, plus 4 percent more on income above about $1.08 million. South Carolina&#39;s top rate is 5.21 percent. On rates alone this is the closest matchup of any state we cover.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina gives a couple a deduction of up to $30,000 where Massachusetts has none, only an $8,800 personal exemption. The South Carolina deduction shrinks above $80,000 of income and is gone at $190,000, so a middle income keeps more here before any rate applies. Massachusetts taxes short-term capital gains at 8.5 percent where South Carolina treats them as ordinary income. The 9 percent top combined rate above the million-dollar line has no counterpart here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">If a business sale, vested stock or a large short-term gain falls in the same year as your move, the timing of the sale against the change of domicile could mean large savings. That is a question for your accountant before you sign anything.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Retirees, both directions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Check your pension type before you decide</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of this page runs in South Carolina&#39;s favor, so read this section twice, because it does not. Massachusetts does not tax its own contributory public pensions: a retired Massachusetts teacher, police officer or state worker collects that pension free of Massachusetts income tax. South Carolina treats it like any other pension. The deduction reduces it: once you are 65, up to $15,000 of income per person is deducted, and the retirement income deduction of up to $10,000 counts against that same $15,000 rather than adding to it. Above that amount, the pension is taxed here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a couple with one large Massachusetts public pension, that single line can offset much of what the property tax gives back. It does not usually make South Carolina the more expensive state, because the house, the heat and the property tax all still cost less here. It narrows the saving, and nobody should discover that in April. Run both states against your actual pension before you list the house; the <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">calculator</a> takes a minute and we will happily check the arithmetic with you.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Everything else lands the usual way. Social Security is untaxed in both states. Private pensions, 401(k) and IRA withdrawals face the Massachusetts flat rate with no senior exclusion, against South Carolina&#39;s deductions. Military retirement is fully exempt here at any age, which Massachusetts also honors. The <a href="/buyers/retirees/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">retiree page</a> covers what else changes at this stage of the move.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Retirees, both directions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Check your pension type before you decide</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of this page runs in South Carolina&#39;s favor. This section does not. Massachusetts does not tax its own contributory public pensions: a retired Massachusetts teacher, police officer or state worker collects that pension free of Massachusetts income tax. South Carolina treats it like any other pension. The deduction reduces it: once you are 65, up to $15,000 of income per person is deducted, and the retirement income deduction of up to $10,000 counts against that same $15,000 rather than adding to it. Above that amount, the pension is taxed here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a couple with one large Massachusetts public pension, that single line can offset much of what the property tax gives back. It does not usually make South Carolina the more expensive state, because the house, the heat and the property tax all still cost less here. It narrows the saving, and nobody should discover that in April. Run both states against your actual pension before you list the house; the <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">calculator</a> takes a minute and we will happily check the arithmetic with you.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Everything else lands the usual way. Social Security is untaxed in both states. Private pensions, 401(k) and IRA withdrawals face the Massachusetts flat rate with no senior exclusion, against South Carolina&#39;s deductions. Military retirement is fully exempt here at any age, which Massachusetts also honors. The <a href="/buyers/retirees/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">retiree page</a> covers what else changes at this stage of the move.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The rest of the bill</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The property tax and the car excise both fall here</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Property tax</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Massachusetts averages about 1 percent effective with a median bill of $6,080, the bill pushed up by the values underneath it. Horry County&#39;s primary-home rate works out near 0.38 percent and the median South Carolina bill is $1,337. You apply for the primary rate yourself by May 31; the <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">property tax page</a> has the calculator and the deadline.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">The car excise you already pay</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No warning needed here either. Massachusetts bills $25 per $1,000 of valuation, the same 2.5 percent in every town. Horry County taxes vehicles on value too, and the working bill is smaller for most movers. It is about $362 a year on a $30,000 car in the unincorporated county, or about $458 inside the City of Myrtle Beach, plus a $50 road fee. A one-time $250 fee applies when an out-of-state title first registers. The <a href="/buyers/relocating/moving-checklist/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">checklist</a> has the 45-day order of operations.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Sales tax rises</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Massachusetts charges 6.25 percent and exempts groceries and clothing under $175. Here it is 8 percent in the county and 9 percent inside the City of Myrtle Beach, and we would rather be honest with you about that: on most purchases the tax is higher. The grocery run is the exception both ways: groceries are untaxed in Horry County and everyday goods price lower here than in Massachusetts on the federal price index, so the cart itself usually costs less. Clothing loses its exemption, which is fair to know before the first back-to-school shopping.</p></div></section>
 
