-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Getting around Myrtle Beach FAQ</h2><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Does Myrtle Beach have an airport?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes, inside the city. Myrtle Beach International had ten airlines and nonstop service to more than 50 cities as of August 2026, and 3.8 million passengers in 2024, the last full year it has published. A terminal expansion opened in December 2025.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Do you need a car in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Plan on one. The public bus runs seven days a week and covers the main towns, but routes are limited and most daily life here is built around driving. Many neighborhoods also allow golf carts on local streets, with a permit.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Is there public transportation in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Coast RTA runs ten fixed bus routes seven days a week, 362 days a year, serving Myrtle Beach, Conway, Surfside Beach, Garden City, Murrells Inlet, Pawleys Island, Georgetown, Loris and more. Fares are about a dollar a ride; check the current table on their site. Every bus is wheelchair accessible.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(-