 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/hurricanes/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Do hurricanes hit Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Since 1989, seven hurricanes and one tropical storm have done real damage on the Grand Strand, from Hugo through Tropical Storm Debby in 2024. Most seasons pass with no direct hit. The Atlantic season runs June 1 to November 30 and peaks around September 10."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When was the last hurricane to hit Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Hurricane Ian made landfall near Georgetown as a Category 1 on September 30, 2022, damaging three piers here and pushing sand over roads in Garden City. Tropical Storm Debby followed in August 2024 with 15 to 19 inches of rain and river flooding that lasted well after the storm."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What was the worst hurricane in Myrtle Beach history?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Hugo, September 22, 1989, a Category 4 that came ashore near Sullivan's Island. Weather service records show a 13 foot storm surge at Garden City that destroyed many homes there, and three piers destroyed in Myrtle Beach. Nothing since has matched it. Our Broker-in-Charge was here for Hugo; his first person account is on this page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Myrtle Beach flood?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two different ways. Storm surge floods low ground near the ocean during a storm. River flooding comes days later and reaches inland: after Florence in 2018 the Waccamaw River crested at 21.16 feet and flooded about 1,000 homes and businesses near Conway, plus 1,580 more in rural parts of the county. Check the flood zone of the address, not the town name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When is hurricane season in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"June 1 to November 30, the Atlantic season. Government climatology puts the peak at September 10, with most activity from mid August to mid October. Every damaging storm in the local record since 1989 arrived between August and early October."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is my hurricane evacuation zone in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Horry County uses lettered Zones A, B and C, and evacuations are ordered by zone. Look up any address on the state's Know Your Zone map before you buy, the same way you check its flood zone. We pull both for every address our buyers consider."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do streets flood in Myrtle Beach when it rains?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"During hard rain some streets hold a few inches of water, and the main roads usually drain within hours of the rain stopping. Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach have spent tens of millions on deepwater ocean outfalls that carry street runoff offshore. The exceptions: the lowest streets near the beach when a high tide blocks the drains, and river neighborhoods, where flooding can last days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a king tide in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The informal name for the highest tides of the year, on predictable dates around fall full moons. A few low streets flood with no storm at all, most visibly on Pawleys Island, where the causeways have been closed at peak tide and the town is funding a drainage plan. If you are buying on a low street, ask us for the tide dates before you tour."}}]}</script>
