-<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/">Buyers</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/relocating/">Relocating</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Hurricanes</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">The storm record</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Hurricanes in Myrtle Beach:<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">every damaging storm since 1989.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 1, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Every hurricane to reach the Grand Strand since Hugo in 1989 and what each one did, plus what to check on any Myrtle Beach address before you buy.</p><div style="margin-top:1.8rem;display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="#lead-form">Ask about the storms</a><a class="btn btn-outline btn-lg" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div>
+<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/">Buyers</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/relocating/">Relocating</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Hurricanes</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">The storm record</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Hurricanes in Myrtle Beach:<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">every damaging storm since 1989.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 9, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Yes. Eight storms have done real damage here since 1989. The last major hurricane to come ashore was Hugo. South Carolina is hit least of the five most-hit states.</p><div style="margin-top:1.8rem;display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="#lead-form">Ask about the storms</a><a class="btn btn-outline btn-lg" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Myrtle Beach can have hurricanes</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Start with the comparison, because it is the fairest way to frame the risk. Of the five states hurricanes hit most, South Carolina is hit the least. Since 1851 the federal count of direct hurricane hits is 120 for Florida, 64 for Texas, 56 for North Carolina and 54 for Louisiana, against 30 here. The last major hurricane to come ashore on this coast was Hugo, in 1989.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We are not going to tell you hurricanes are not a thing here, because they are, and we are not going to tell you they are constant, because the record says otherwise. Since 1989, eight tropical systems have done real damage on the Grand Strand. Most seasons pass without a direct hit. Every damaging storm in that record arrived between August and early October.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The Atlantic season runs June 1 to November 30. Government climatology puts the peak at September 10, with most activity between mid August and mid October. What follows is each storm and what it did here, from National Weather Service storm reports. You can then judge the risk from facts instead of from a brochure in either direction.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">How often do hurricanes hit Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Since 1851 the federal count of direct hurricane hits is 30 for South Carolina, against 120 for Florida, 64 for Texas, 56 for North Carolina and 54 for Louisiana. Of the five states hurricanes hit most, South Carolina is hit the least. The last major hurricane to come ashore on this coast was Hugo, in 1989.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We are not going to tell you hurricanes are not a thing here, because they are, and we are not going to tell you they are constant, because the record says otherwise. Since 1989, eight tropical systems have done real damage on the Grand Strand. Most seasons pass without a direct hit. Every damaging storm in that record arrived between August and early October.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The Atlantic season runs June 1 to November 30. Government climatology puts the peak at September 10, with most activity between mid August and mid October. What follows is each storm and what it did here, from National Weather Service storm reports. You can then judge the risk from facts instead of from a brochure in either direction.</p></div></section>
 
