 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">No storm required</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">King tides, and the few low streets they reach</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A king tide is the informal name for the highest tides of the year. They arrive on predictable dates, mostly around fall full moons, with no storm attached. On those days salt water can come up through the storm drains on the lowest streets until the tide turns.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Pawleys Island is the local example. On the biggest tides the island&#39;s low streets flood, and the causeways have been closed at the peak until the water dropped. The town says so itself: officials call flooding their first public safety issue, and the island is funding a drainage and flood plan. People who live there check the dates and plan around them. If you are shopping on Pawleys, the tide calendar comes with the island; ask us and we will time a showing to a high tide so you see the street at its worst before you offer.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Cherry Grove and the lowest streets of Garden City see the same tides.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">From our team</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What we do when a warning goes up</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a tropical storm or a low-end hurricane, our team stays home and waits it out. Many houses here have window protectors that fold down over the glass in a few minutes, and you will see the mounts beside the windows when you tour. Debby in 2024 was that kind of storm for us: days of rain and flooded rivers rather than destructive wind on the beach.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">During one stronger storm, someone we know boarded up his house and took his family to sleep at his office for the night, because the building is newer commercial construction and he trusted it more than his house. Calm and specific. Water, fuel, the shutters down, and a decision made early about staying or going.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">When the county orders an evacuation for your zone, you go. That is what the lettered zones below are for, and the county announces orders zone by zone on its own channels. Everything above is our experience, not safety instruction. The county&#39;s emergency division decides.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">From our team</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What we do when a warning goes up</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a tropical storm or a low-end hurricane, our team stays home and waits it out. Many houses here have window protectors that fold down over the glass in a few minutes, and you will see the mounts beside the windows when you tour. Debby in 2024 was that kind of storm for us: days of rain and flooded rivers rather than destructive wind on the beach.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a stronger storm, the plan is water, fuel, the shutters down, and a decision made early about staying or going.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">When the county orders an evacuation for your zone, you go. That is what the lettered zones below are for, and the county announces orders zone by zone on its own channels. Everything above is our experience, not safety instruction. The county&#39;s emergency division decides.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Before you buy</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Three things to check on any address, before you fall for the house</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">The flood zone</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Look the address up on the federal flood maps. The zone decides whether a lender requires flood insurance and is the single best public clue to where water has gone before. We pull it for every address our buyers consider.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">The evacuation zone</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County uses lettered evacuation zones, A, B and C, and the state orders evacuations by zone. The <a href="https://www.scemd.org/prepare/know-your-zone/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Know Your Zone map</a> shows any address in seconds. Knowing your letter before a storm is the difference between following one clear instruction and refreshing the news.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Insurance can pause while a storm approaches</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">An insurance company may place a moratorium on issuing new policies in an area where a hurricane is expected. That pause matters most when you are under contract: the loan cannot close without a bound policy, so a buyer who leaves insurance to the week of closing can end up waiting out a storm instead. Bind the policy early in the process, and we get estimates on the exact address as part of any offer we write. A storm plan you made in February beats one you make in September.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Want the flood zone, evacuation zone and insurance picture on a house?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Ask us about an address</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section>
 
