 <section id="dmv" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Week one</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Insurance, then the county, then the DMV: the order matters</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina charges an annual property tax on cars, paid a year in advance to the county, and the DMV will not register a vehicle until it is paid. The sequence is fixed. Get South Carolina auto insurance. Take each vehicle&#39;s registration or title and proof of your Horry County address to the county Auditor, who writes the vehicle tax notice. Pay it at the county Treasurer. Take the paid receipt to the DMV.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Myrtle Beach the county Auditor and Treasurer are at 1201 21st Avenue North and the DMV branch is at 1200 21st Avenue North, across the street. In Little River the county office and the DMV share 107 Highway 57 North. Conway has the county offices at 1301 Second Avenue and the DMV at 4103 Highway 701 North. Offices run weekdays; the DMV opens at 8:30 and at 9:30 on Wednesdays. There is no DMV branch in Surfside, Socastee, or Carolina Forest.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">What each vehicle costs</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At the DMV: a one-time $250 infrastructure maintenance fee, a $15 title fee, and $40 for a plate and registration that lasts two years. That is $305 per standard passenger vehicle. The $250 fee is South Carolina&#39;s version of vehicle sales tax, and you pay it once, on arrival, in place of tax on a car you already own. Owners 65 and older pay $36 for the plate instead of $40.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At the county: the vehicle tax, which New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Ohio do not charge and which surprises almost everyone who arrives from them. It is not only cars: the county also bills boats and campers every year, so count them when you budget. Vehicles are assessed at 6 percent of value and then taxed at the local millage, and it comes due every year with the plate renewal. Horry County publishes a <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/vehicle-tax/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">vehicle tax estimator</a> on its vehicle tax page. A high-mileage discount exists, but only in the month the tax is due, so ask the Auditor about it at the counter.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">The license itself</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Same visit if you brought the paperwork above. An eight-year license is $25. Expect a vision test; if your old license has been expired nine months or more, expect the knowledge and road tests too. Military members and students living here temporarily can keep their home-state license.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Buying before you move? We can time the closing around these dates.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Ask about my move</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section>
 
-<section id="residence" style="background:var(--ivory-2);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The one that saves the most</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">File the 4 percent legal residence application by May 31</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">This is general information, not tax or legal advice.</strong> Deadlines and required documents change. Confirm your own situation with the Horry County Auditor or a tax professional before you rely on any of it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County taxes an owner-occupied home at a 4 percent assessment and everything else at 6 percent, and the 4 percent rate also removes the school operating tax. On the same house the difference is roughly three to one. Nobody applies for you. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property tax page</a> has the calculator and the full explanation. This section covers the deadline and the documents.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Apply online through the county&#39;s tax-payer services page. It asks for three things. A South Carolina driver&#39;s license or state ID for every owner-occupant and their spouse, showing the new address. South Carolina registration for every vehicle you own, showing the new address. Redacted copies of your most recent federal and state income tax returns, or an SSA-1099. If you still own a home in another state, you also send that county&#39;s tax bill showing no owner-occupied exemption there. That is why the license and registration come first: they are attachments to this form.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County asks for the application by May 31 so the bill mailed in the fall prints at 4 percent. File later and you can still qualify for that year, but you will be dealing with a corrected bill or a refund instead of a right bill the first time.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Homestead exemption at 65: not in year one</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">South Carolina exempts the first $50,000 of a legal residence&#39;s value for owners who are 65 or older, totally and permanently disabled, or legally blind. It requires the 4 percent status and one full year as a South Carolina legal resident as of December 31, so a retiree who moves this year applies for it next year, at the county Auditor.</p></div></section>
