hey are attachments to this form.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County asks for the application by May 31 so the bill mailed in the fall prints at 4 percent. File later and you can still qualify for that year, but you will be dealing with a corrected bill or a refund instead of a right bill the first time.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Homestead exemption at 65: not in year one</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">South Carolina exempts the first $50,000 of a legal residence&#39;s value for owners who are 65 or older, totally and permanently disabled, or legally blind. It requires the 4 percent status and one full year as a South Carolina legal resident as of December 31, so a retiree who moves this year applies for it next year, at the county Auditor.</p></div></section>
 
-<section id="utilities" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Utilities</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Who provides what depends on which side of a line you are on</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">There is no single utility for the Grand Strand. Which company serves your house depends on the address, and neither electric provider publishes a street-level map. The fastest way to find out is to ask the seller for a recent bill or ask the closing attorney. Failing that, call and give them the address.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Electricity</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two providers cover almost all of Horry County. Santee Cooper, the state-owned utility, serves the City of Myrtle Beach and the other coastal municipalities. Horry Electric Cooperative, which is member-owned, serves most of the unincorporated county, including Carolina Forest, Socastee, Longs, and the rural areas.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Both run a credit check and may want a deposit. Santee Cooper sets its deposit from the two highest consecutive bills at that address, with a floor of $100, and refunds it after 13 months of on-time payments. A prepaid option costs $70 up front and skips the deposit and credit check entirely. Horry Electric charges $35 in one-time fees to open an account, a $5 membership, a $10 application and a $20 connection, and adds a deposit only if the credit check calls for one. Both want proof you own or rent the place, so have the settlement statement or lease handy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Know about the demand charge before your first bill arrives. On their published 2026 residential schedules, both Santee Cooper and Horry Electric Cooperative bill a demand charge as well as the power you use. That means part of your bill is set by your single highest hour of use rather than your monthly total. The peak window is 3 p.m. to 6 p.m. from April through October and 6 a.m. to 9 a.m. from November through March. Running several heavy appliances at once inside that window sets a charge that applies to the whole month, so it is worth spreading the load. Our <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">cost of living page</a> has the rates.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Water and sewer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Outside the city limits, most of the coast is served by Grand Strand Water &amp; Sewer Authority, which asks for 24 hours notice before it turns anything on. Apply on a Friday and you are waiting until Monday, so make this call before the truck arrives, not after. Its new service fee is $40, and deposits run $40 to $90 for owners and $60 to $140 for tenants depending on whether you need water, sewer, or both.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside city limits you deal with the city. Myrtle Beach bills water, sewer, sanitation, and stormwater on one monthly statement and sets the deposit by meter size. North Myrtle Beach charges a meter deposit, $180 for the common three-quarter-inch meter, plus a $50 service charge. Conway runs its own utility billing. On the Georgetown side, Pawleys Island and Litchfield are served by the Georgetown County Water and Sewer District.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Gas, internet, and the rest</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Natural gas is not everywhere. Dominion Energy is the gas utility, but the mains reach only some neighborhoods, so availability is checked address by address. Plenty of homes here are all electric or run propane, which matters if you were planning on a gas range.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">For internet, HTC is a member-owned fiber cooperative covering much of the county, with published starting prices around $50 for 500 Mbps and about $75 for a gig. Spectrum is the incumbent cable provider and advertises lower first-year promotional rates that step up afterward. Brightspeed has fiber in parts of the city. Budget roughly $40 to $75 a month depending on the speed you need and whether you are still inside a first-year promotion.</p></div></section>
