m 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only when the home is rented out. The IRS lists homeowners association (HOA) fees, condo fees and common charges with homeowners insurance, utilities and internet service as costs you <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p530" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cannot deduct on a home</a>. On a rental, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Publication 527</a> lets you deduct dues paid for upkeep of the shared parts of a condo building.</p><figure class="tx-fig tx-rulefig"><p class="cap">Are the HOA dues deductible? It depends on how the home is used</p><table class="tx-rules"><thead><tr><th scope="col">How you use the home</th><th scope="col">Deductible?</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">You live in it all year</th><td data-label="Deductible?"><span class="tx-a no">No</span></td><td data-label="What it means">HOA fees on a home you live in are a personal cost, like your insurance and utilities.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A second home you never rent</th><td data-label="Deductible?"><span class="tx-a no">No</span></td><td data-label="What it means">The same rule as a main home. The dues are not deductible.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You rent it out and never stay there</th><td data-label="Deductible?"><span class="tx-a yes">Yes, all</span></td><td data-label="What it means">All of the dues are a rental expense, including months it sits empty while you hold it out for rent (listed and open to bookings).</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You rent it out and stay there some nights</th><td data-label="Deductible?"><span class="tx-a part">Partly</span></td><td data-label="What it means">You deduct the rented share of the dues, worked out by nights.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Your home, rented fewer than 15 days</th><td data-label="Deductible?"><span class="tx-a no">No</span></td><td data-label="What it means">You report no rent and deduct no rental costs.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You work from home</th><td data-label="Deductible?"><span class="tx-a no">Not for employees</span></td><td data-label="What it means">An employee gets no home office deduction. A self-employed owner who qualifies splits whole-home costs by the share used for business.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A deduction is not money back. It lowers the rental income that is taxed, and what it saves depends on the rest of your return.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="rented-share"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Your rented share">How much of the HOA fee can I deduct if I rent and use the condo?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You deduct the rented share: rented nights as a share of rented nights plus personal nights. Nights the condo sat empty count on neither side.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A night rented at a fair price counts as rented, even if you were there that day. A fair price is about what a person not related to you would pay. A rent far below what similar condos nearby charge is not a fair price.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Put in your own numbers:</p><figure class="tx-cfig"><div class="tx-calc" role="group" aria-labelledby="txcalc-h"><p class="k" id="txcalc-h">Work out your deductible dues</p><div class="f"><label for="txDues">HOA dues a month</label><span class="in">$ <input id="txDues" type="number" min="0" step="25" value="650" inputmode="numeric"></span></div><div class="f"><label for="txRent">Nights rented at a fair price this year</label><span class="in"><input id="txRent" type="number" min="0" max="365" step="1" value="120" inputmode="numeric"></span></div><div class="f"><label for="txMine">Your personal nights this year</label><span class="in"><input id="txMine" type="number" min="0" max="365" step="1" value="20" inputmode="numeric"></span></div><div class="out"><span>Dues you deduct for the year</span><strong id="txDed" aria-live="polite">$6,686</strong></div><div class="out s"><span>Rented share of the nights</span><strong id="txShare">86%</strong></div><p id="txSay" class="say">This condo counts as your home under the IRS test below, because your personal nights are more than 14. The figure above is the most you can deduct. It is cut if your rental costs are more than the rent.</p></div></figure><script>(function(){var D=function deduct(monthly, rented, personal) {
