 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"When is the Horry County foreclosure sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At 11 in the morning on the first Monday of each month, or the first Tuesday when the Monday is a holiday. It is held on the third floor of the Horry County Government and Justice Building, 1301 2nd Avenue, Conway. The list is on the county website."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much money do I need at the foreclosure sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Certified funds. A first-time winner brings a $2,500 certified check to the close of bidding. Every winner pays 5 percent of the bid by noon the next day and the balance within 30 days. A winner who does not pay forfeits the deposit and the right to bid at future sales."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can someone outbid me after the sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, when the lender asked for a deficiency judgment. Bidding stays open for 30 days and reopens at 11 in the morning on the 30th day. Anyone can bid more, and the lender cannot bid again. When the lender waived the deficiency, the sale closes on sale day."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the master's deed clear all the liens?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. It carries no warranty. Liens held by parties to the case end at the sale. Taxes and any lien held by someone not in the case survive, and unpaid taxes fall on the bidder."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get a mortgage for a foreclosure auction purchase?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The county terms give you 30 days and no contingency. Ask the lender before the sale whether it can close inside that window on a deed with no warranty. Bank-owned listings take ordinary financing, and a loan on one is about 45 days in Chapter3's experience."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an investor buy a HUD home?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only in the extended period, after the owner-occupant exclusive period of 15 days for an insured home or five days for an uninsured one. The offer goes through a HUD-registered broker, and the home sells as-is."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a family member pay the taxes and take the house after a tax sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The owner, a grantee from the owner, or a mortgage or judgment creditor can redeem within 12 months, and redemption puts the owner back in place. A relative who pays does not take title."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a tax deed good title?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The statute makes it evidence of good title, not a warranty. A suit to recover the land can be brought within two years of the sale. Until then the title is contestable, and a sale or a loan can wait on a quiet title action."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who holds the earnest money on a bank-owned purchase?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The closing attorney, as in any sale here. The rest of the terms are negotiated in the contract."}}]}</script>
