s automatically so the signal is in front of you."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Should I pay cash or finance?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Borrowed money can help or hurt: financing raises cash-on-cash return when the spread between rent yield and rate is positive, and lowers it when the spread is negative. A common Grand Strand path is closing with a DSCR loan, then refinancing after rents season or renovations raise value. Cash still wins for speed and for condotels."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I still invest here if my property can’t be a short-term rental?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, and this is one of the most useful workarounds on the Grand Strand. Many Myrtle Beach residential zones (and plenty of HOAs) don’t allow Airbnb-style short-term rentals. South Carolina’s 90-day threshold is a tax rule: stays under 90 days are subject to the state accommodations tax. It does not override local zoning. Cities, counties and HOAs set their own minimum lease terms, and those minimums vary, so a 90-day lease is not automatically permitted where nightly rentals are banned. Mid-term tenants such as traveling nurses, remote workers and October-to-March snowbirds are a common way to cash-flow a property in those zones. Before you count on it, confirm the minimum lease term in that city’s ordinance and the HOA covenants for the specific address. Run any address through the analyzer to compare the long-term and mid-term numbers."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What does the long-term rental analyzer calculate?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "For any Grand Strand address you enter, it estimates property value, market rent, DSCR, cap rate, net operating income, monthly cash flow, cash-on-cash return, and appreciation, and it flags nearby permit activity."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is DSCR and why does it matter?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Debt-Service Coverage Ratio is net operating income divided by annual debt service. Investor lenders typically look for 1.20 or higher. It tells you whether the rent comfortably covers the loan."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What cap rate is realistic for a Myrtle Beach long-term rental?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "As of July 2026, a Horry County rental bought in the cheaper third of the market returns 4.2 to 7.5 percent a year after costs with a property manager, and 5.1 to 8.8 percent without one. Dividing the same rent by the typical value of every home in the area gives a much lower number, because most of those homes are not rentals. The rental returns page has the figure for each area."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Are the numbers an appraisal?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. They are an actual screening estimate to compare deals quickly. Confirm with comparable sales, inspections, and a licensed agent before making an offer."}}]}</script>
