olid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$320,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$1,380</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>1.8%</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">2.3%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">One house, line by line</h3><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Line</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">A year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Rent, 12 months at $1,823</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$21,876</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Empty months and repairs, 10 percent</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">−$2,188</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Management, 10 percent</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">−$2,188</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Property tax at 6 percent, 254.6 mills</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">−$2,455</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Landlord insurance</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">−$3,050</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Rent left</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>$11,996</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Divided by the $161,000 price</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>7.5 percent</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Property tax is the biggest cost after the allowance. A rental pays the 6 percent ratio, not the 4 percent a home you live in pays. <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The property tax page</a> has every district's rate, and <a href="/invest/landlord-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the insurance page</a> has the range.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The 10 percent allowance for empty months and repairs is close to what the market runs. <a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SCRVAC" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina's rental vacancy rate</a> was 9.8 percent in 2025. A lender is stricter and <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-3.8-02/rental-income-subject-property" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">counts only 75 percent of the rent</a> when it decides your loan.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-return-does-a-nightly-rental-make-in-each-area"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What return does a nightly rental make in each area?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every bar here is the same house. The brass bar is that house on a yearly lease. The two green bars are the same house rented nightly: first at the occupancy its market averages, then at 60 percent, which is what a well-run listing books.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://www.airroi.com/airbnb-data/united-states/south-carolina/myrtle-beach" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The revenue and occupancy are AirROI's average per active listing</a> in each market for the 12 months to September 2026, before costs. The revenue includes the cleaning fees guests pay. Nightly costs take 55 percent of revenue, covering management, cleaning, utilities, platform fees, the 6 percent tax and insurance. What Myrtle Beach Airbnbs gross, by area and month, is on <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our Airbnb income page</a>.</p><div style="max-width:760px;margin:1.1rem 0 1.3rem"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 446" role="img" aria-label="The same house let by the year, let nightly at the market's average occupancy, and let nightly at 60 percent occupancy" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;font-family:var(--sans)"><line x1="208" y1="18" x2="208" y2="404" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.13)"/><text x="208" y="420" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(28,32,40,.72)">0%</text><line x1="304" y1="18" x2="304" y2="404" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.13)"/><text x="304" y="420" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(28,32,40,.72)">2%</text><line x1="399" y1="18" x2="399" y2="404" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.13)"/><text x="399" y="420" font-size="12