00}main#main .ev-plans.d .ev-card::before{background:var(--ev-d)}main#main .ev-plans.r .ev-card::before{background:var(--ev-r)}main#main .ev-plans.d .ev-pill{color:var(--ev-d)}main#main .ev-plans.r .ev-pill{color:var(--ev-r)}main#main .ev-seats{position:relative;display:flex;height:2rem;margin:.9rem 0 .4rem;font:700 .92rem/2rem var(--sans);color:#fff}main#main .ev-seats .r{background:var(--ev-r);padding-left:.6rem}main#main .ev-seats .d{background:var(--ev-d);text-align:right;padding-right:.6rem}main#main .ev-seats i{position:absolute;top:-.35rem;bottom:-.35rem;width:3px;background:var(--ev-ink)}main#main .ev-meter .k{font-size:.95rem;color:var(--ev-slate)}main#main .ev-meter{display:block}main#main .ev-meter .top{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem}</style><div class="ev-dash"><div class="ev-tile now"><span class="b">Dec. 11</span><span class="l"><span data-ev-days="2026-12-11">Flood insurance deadline</span></span></div><div class="ev-tile idea"><span class="b">$<span data-ev-count="1780">1,780</span></span><span class="l">Less tax a year on a $350,000 rental at 4 percent</span></div><div class="ev-tile idea"><span class="b"><span data-ev-count="4">4</span> votes</span><span class="l">Short in the state House for that cut</span></div><div class="ev-tile "><span class="b">+<span data-ev-count="0.8" data-ev-dec="1">0.8</span>%</span><span class="l">Home price appreciation here in the latest year</span></div></div><nav class="ev-nav" aria-label="On this page"><ul class="ev-jump"><li class="k">Jump to</li><li><a href="#for-you">Your situation</a></li><li><a href="#rental-tax">Rental tax</a></li><li><a href="#buyer-credit">Buyer credit</a></li><li><a href="#dates">Key dates</a></li><li><a href="#prices">Prices</a></li><li><a href="#plans">Both plans</a></li><li><a href="#election-faq">Election FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Chief Operating Officer, and <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Abdulla Hijazi</strong>, Chief Marketing Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 5, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">In the 2026 election's unofficial count, <span class="ev-dn">Annie Andrews (D)</span> won the U.S. Senate race and <span class="ev-rn">Alan Wilson (R)</span> won for governor of South Carolina.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two dates after the election matter for a Myrtle Beach home this winter. On Dec. 11, federal flood insurance can lapse and stall closings in flood zones, and on Jan. 7, companies with 350 or more houses may no longer buy more.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The bigger changes are proposals that still need votes. Wilson wants a lower property tax on rentals and second homes, and Andrews backs a $50,000 tax credit for first-time buyers.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="for-you"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Your situation">What does the 2026 election change for my home?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two things are set to happen for a Myrtle Beach home, and the rest are proposals. Pick your situation to see what applies to you.</p><p class="ev-key"><span class="now">Set to happen</span><span class="idea">A proposal, not law</span><span>No change</span></p><div data-ev-tabs><div class="ev-tabs n4" role="tablist" aria-label="Pick your situation"><button type="button" role="tab" id="evr-t0" aria-controls="evr-p0" aria-selected="true">I'm buying</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="evr-t1" aria-controls="evr-p1" aria-selected="false">I'm selling</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="evr-t2" aria-controls="evr-p2" aria-selected="false">I own a rental or second home</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="evr-t3" aria-controls="evr-p3" aria-selected="false">I'm building or buying new</button></div><div class="ev-panel" role="tabpanel" id="evr-p0" aria-labelledby="evr-t0"><h3>If you are buying</h3><div class="ev-grid"><div class="ev-card now ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Dec. 11</span><strong class="t">Flood insurance can lapse</strong><p>If Congress does not extend the federal flood program by Dec. 11, it cannot write new policies. A loan on a house in a high-risk flood zone can then wait.</p><p>Our agents check a house's flood zone and its tax ratio before you make an offer. <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What a lapse does</a></p></div><div class="ev-card idea ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Proposal</span><strong class="t">A $50,000 first-time buyer credit</strong><p>Annie Andrews backs one. It has had no vote in Congress, so plan a purchase this winter without it. <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What the last credit did</a></p></div><div class="ev-card now ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Jan. 7</span><strong class="t">Big investors hit a limit</strong><p>A company that controls 350 or more houses may not buy more, with some exceptions. A family buying one rental is not affected.</p></div><div class="ev-card  ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">No change</span><strong class="t">A rental or second home pays the higher tax</strong><p>It is taxed on 6 percent of its value. A home you live in is taxed on 4 percent. Price a second home on today's bill.</p></div></div></div><div class="ev-panel" role="tabpanel" id="evr-p1" aria-labelledby="evr-t1"><h3>If you are selling</h3><div class="ev-grid"><div class="ev-card  ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">No change</span><strong class="t">Prices are about flat</strong><p>Prices here rose about 0.8 percent in the latest year. We price your home from this year's sales of homes like it. <a href="#prices" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Prices after past elections</a></p></div><div class="ev-card now ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Dec. 11</span><strong class="t">A buyer's closing can slip</strong><p>If your house is in a high-risk flood zone and your buyer needs a loan, a lapse can delay the closing. A private flood policy can count if it meets federal rules.</p></div><div class="ev-card  ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">No change</span><strong class="t">The tax on your gain</strong><p>Up to $250,000 of gain on a home you lived in stays out of federal income tax, or $500,000 for certain married couples. Neither winner's plan changes it.</p></div><div class="ev-card idea ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Proposal</span><strong class="t">Lower taxes for rental buyers</strong><p>If the tax on rentals fell, a buyer of a rental or second home would pay less each year. <a href="#rental-tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Try the tax slider</a></p></div></div></div><div class="ev-panel" role="tabpanel" id="evr-p2" aria-labelledby="evr-t2"><h3>If you own a rental or second home</h3><div class="ev-grid"><div class="ev-card idea ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Proposal</span><strong class="t">A lower property tax</strong><p>Alan Wilson wants to cut the higher tax on rentals. At 4 percent, a $350,000 rental in Myrtle Beach would pay about $1,780 less a year. <a href="#rental-tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Try your price</a></p></div><div class="ev-card  ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">No change</span><strong class="t">Flood premiums keep rising</strong><p>On a second home with a discounted federal flood rate, the rate rises 25 percent a year until it reaches the full-risk rate. That is current law.</p></div><div class="ev-card  ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">No change</span><strong class="t">Short-term rental rules</strong><p>We found no statement on short-term rentals from either winner. City zoning still decides where they are allowed. <a href="/invest/short-term-rental-rules-statehouse/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What the statehouse could change</a></p></div><div class="ev-card now ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Jan. 7</span><strong class="t">The investor limit</strong><p>It applies only to companies with 350 or more houses. Andrews wants it cut to 50.</p></div></div></div><div class="ev-panel" role="tabpanel" id="evr-p3" aria-labelledby="evr-t3"><h3>If you are building or buying new</h3><div class="ev-grid"><div class="ev-card idea ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Proposal</span><strong class="t">Faster permits</strong><p>Wilson wants building permits for new homes to move faster. Horry County issued permits for 6,418 new single-family homes in 2025. <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Building in Horry County</a></p></div><div class="ev-card idea ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Proposal</span><strong class="t">Cheaper materials and builder tax cuts</strong><p>Andrews wants to end tariffs the president imposes alone, naming building materials, and to cut taxes for home builders.</p></div><div class="ev-card  ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">No change</span><strong class="t">Impact fees</strong><p>A new home of about 1,500 square feet pays $1,615 in county impact fees. County Council decides them, not the new governor or senator.</p></div><div class="ev-card idea ev-rise"><span class="ev-pill">Proposal</span><strong class="t">Faster roads, and I-73</strong><p>Both winners talk about roads. Funding for most of I-73's $1.6 billion cost has not been identified.</p></div></div></div></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Ask what the election changes for your house</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the address. We tell you its flood zone, its tax ratio and what a pending vote could change for it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask about your house</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="rental-tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Rental tax">How much would a Myrtle Beach rental save if Wilson cuts the rental tax?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About a third of its yearly property tax, if rentals were taxed at 4 percent instead of 6. A home you live in is taxed on 4 percent of its value, and skips the school operating tax. A rental or second home is taxed on 6 percent.</p><figure class="ev-calc" id="evCalc"><div class="k">The tax on a rental inside Myrtle Beach</div><label for="evPrice">The county's value of the home<span class="val" id="evV">$350,000</span></label><input id="evPrice" type="range" min="150000" max="1000000" step="10000" value="350000" aria-label="The county's value of the home, in dollars"><div class="ev-pre" role="group" aria-label="Quick prices"><button type="button" data-v="250000" aria-pressed="false">$250k</button><button type="button" data-v="350000" aria-pressed="true">$350k</button><button type="button" data-v="500000" aria-pressed="false">$500k</button><button type="button" data-v="750000" aria-pressed="false">$750k</button></div><div class="ev-bar"><div class="lab"><span>Today, taxed at 6 percent</span><b id="evSix">$5,350</b></div><div class="tr"><div class="fl" id="evSixB" style="width:35.0%"></div></div></div><div class="ev-bar a"><div class="lab"><span>If rentals moved to 4 percent</span><b id="evFour">$3,560</b></div><div class="tr"><div class="fl" id="evFourB" style="width:23.3%"></div></div></div><div class="ev-bar c"><div class="lab"><span>Taxed like a home you live in</span><b id="evHome">$2,040</b></div><div class="tr"><div class="fl" id="evHomeB" style="width:13.3%"></div></div></div><p class="ev-save">A cut would save about <b id="evSave">$1,780 to $3,310</b> a year.</p><p class="n">Yearly tax, from Horry County's 2026 tax rates for the county, schools and the city of Myrtle Beach. Before credits, fees and any special district.</p></figure><table class="ev-tab"><caption>Yearly tax on a rental inside Myrtle Beach</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">County's value</th><th scope="col">Today, 6%</th><th scope="col">At 4%</th><th scope="col">Like a home you live in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$250,000</td><td>$3,820</td><td>$2,550</td><td>$1,460</td></tr><tr><td>$350,000</td><td>$5,350</td><td>$3,560</td><td>$2,040</td></tr><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$7,640</td><td>$5,090</td><td>$2,910</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What has to happen first</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The 6 percent is in the <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/scconstitution/A10.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">state constitution</a>. Changing it takes two-thirds of each chamber, so it needs some Democratic votes.</p><div class="ev-meter ev-rise"><div class="top"><span class="big">4</span><p><strong>More House votes needed beyond every Republican.</strong> The change takes 83 of 124 House votes and 31 of 46 Senate votes. Republicans hold 34 Senate seats.</p></div><div class="ev-seats" role="img" aria-label="State House after the election, unofficial count: 79 Republicans and 45 Democrats. Two-thirds is 83 votes."><span class="r" style="width:63.7%">79 R</span><span class="d" style="width:36.3%">45 D</span><i style="left:66.9%"></i></div><p class="k">State House, unofficial count. The black line is 83 votes, two-thirds.</p></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Wilson has published no bill. The other path is a <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/scconstitution/A16.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">constitutional amendment</a>, which voters could approve in November 2028 at the earliest.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where this was tried before, and what it points to</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Act 388, a 2006 state law, took the school operating tax off homes people live in and added a 1 percent sales tax. A 2020 Lincoln Institute study found the state's system shifted the tax burden onto rentals, second homes and businesses. The South Carolina Chamber Foundation and South Carolina Realtors chose its case-study counties. The 2006 law has defenders too, such as nohometax.org.</p><ul class="ev-runs"><li class="ev-run ev-rise"><span class="h">Next 1 to 2 years</span><p>If a cut passes, the bill on a rental here falls by about a third.</p><p>No plan names who makes up the lost tax: other owners, businesses or the state.</p></li><li class="ev-run ev-rise"><span class="h">5 years and more</span><p>A cut would move part of the burden the 2006 law shifted back off rentals.</p><p>No study we found shows whether the saving would reach tenants or show up in prices.</p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More: <a href="/invest/rental-property-tax-cut/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the rental tax cut for investors</a> and <a href="/buyers/act-388-changes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what changing the 2006 school tax law would mean</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="buyer-credit"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Buyer credit">Would a $50,000 first-time buyer credit raise Myrtle Beach home prices?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The last credit raised prices a little and sales a lot. A $50,000 credit has never been tried, so how much more is unknown.</p><figure class="ev-hb"><figcaption class="cap">The largest first-time buyer credit, then and proposed</figcaption><div class="r"><span>2008 to 2010, paid</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl s" style="width:16%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(16% + .45rem)">$8,000</span></div></div><div class="r"><span>Proposed now</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl a" style="width:100%"></div><span class="v in">$50,000</span></div></div><p class="note">The proposed credit is about six times the old one.</p></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">From 2008 to 2010, a federal credit paid first-time buyers up to $8,000. A study in the Journal of Finance found it raised home sales by about 10 percent and prices by about 1 percent. Many buyers simply bought a few years earlier than they would have.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Here it did not stop a fall. Myrtle Beach-area prices dropped about 10 percent in 2009 and kept falling until 2012.</p><ul class="ev-runs"><li class="ev-run ev-rise"><span class="h">Next 1 to 2 years</span><p>If it passes, the same pattern points to more sales and slightly higher prices, about 1 percent the last time.</p><p>Some buyers would move a purchase earlier to get the credit.</p></li><li class="ev-run ev-rise"><span class="h">5 years and more</span><p>Unknown. No study measured a credit this large, or what happens once buyers who bought early are gone.</p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">First it needs a vote in the House and the Senate and the president's signature. It has had no vote. More: <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the first-time buyer tax credit</a>.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-e/election-2026/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-e/election-2026/us-capitol-west-front-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-e/election-2026/us-capitol-west-front-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="622" alt="The west front of the U.S. Capitol, with its white dome between the Senate and House wings, behind a wide green lawn" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Congress-AOC" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Architect of the Capitol. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: Architect of the Capitol</a></span></div><figcaption>The U.S. Capitol, where a buyer credit and the flood insurance deadline are decided.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="dates"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Key dates">What dates matter for a Myrtle Beach home sale after the election?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Dec. 11 matters most. It is the deadline for federal flood insurance, and it comes before the new senator takes office.</p><ol class="ev-time"><li class="ev-rise"><div class="c"><p class="d"><b>Dec. 11, 2026</b><span>Federal flood insurance can lapse</span></p><p>Unless Congress extends it, the program cannot write new policies. A loan on a house in a high-risk flood zone can then wait for the program to return, or for a private policy that meets federal rules.</p><p>It has happened before. In June 2010, over 1,400 closings a day were canceled or delayed across the country, by one estimate. The longest recent lapse ran from Oct. 1 to Nov. 12, 2025. Every lapse so far ended with Congress restoring the program. <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Flood insurance after the election</a></p></div></li><li class="ev-rise"><div class="c"><p class="d"><b>Jan. 3, 2027</b><span>Annie Andrews takes her Senate seat</span></p><p>Her housing plans need a vote of the House and the Senate and the president's signature. The flood deadline comes before she takes office.</p></div></li><li class="ev-rise"><div class="c"><p class="d"><b>Jan. 7, 2027</b><span>The limit on big investors starts</span></p><p>A company that controls 350 or more single-family houses may not buy more, with some exceptions. It became law in July. Andrews wants the number cut to 50.</p></div></li><li class="ev-rise idea"><div class="c"><p class="d"><b>November 2028</b><span>The earliest voters could change the rental tax in the constitution</span></p><p>The other path to a lower rental tax is an amendment to the state constitution. It needs two-thirds of each chamber, then a yes from voters, then a second vote of the legislature.</p></div></li></ol><ul class="ev-runs"><li class="ev-run ev-rise"><span class="h">Next 1 to 2 years</span><p>If the program lapses, closings in high-risk flood zones can wait from a few hours to several weeks, based on past lapses.</p></li><li class="ev-run ev-rise"><span class="h">5 years and more</span><p>Congress has kept the program going with 36 short extensions since 2017. Most premiums can rise up to 18 percent a year by law, whoever is in the Senate.</p></li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices">Will Myrtle Beach home prices go down after the election?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Prices here rose in the year after each of the last five elections, by 3 to 22 percent. They fell only after 2008, in the housing slump.</p><figure class="ev-hb"><figcaption class="cap">Change in Myrtle Beach-area home prices in the year after each election</figcaption><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="After the 2008 election, in the housing slump, prices fell 9.7 percent in a year."><span>2008</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl c" style="width:44.8%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(44.8% + .45rem)">−9.7%</span></div></div><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="After the 2016 presidential election, prices rose 5.6 percent."><span>2016</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl " style="width:26.0%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(26.0% + .45rem)">+5.6%</span></div></div><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="After the 2018 midterm election, prices rose 5.0 percent."><span>2018</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl " style="width:23.2%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(23.2% + .45rem)">+5.0%</span></div></div><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="After the 2020 presidential election, prices rose 21.6 percent, the largest rise."><span>2020</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl " style="width:100.0%"></div><span class="v in">+21.6%</span></div></div><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="After the 2022 midterm election, prices rose 8.5 percent."><span>2022</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl " style="width:39.4%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(39.4% + .45rem)">+8.5%</span></div></div><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="After the 2024 presidential election, prices rose 3.0 percent."><span>2024</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl " style="width:13.8%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(13.8% + .45rem)">+3.0%</span></div></div><div class="r" tabindex="0" role="button" data-say="In the latest year, to mid-2026, prices rose 0.8 percent, about flat."><span>Latest year</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl s" style="width:3.8%"></div><span class="v" style="left:calc(3.8% + .45rem)">+0.8%</span></div></div><p class="ev-read" aria-live="polite">Pick a year to read it in words.</p><p class="note">Federal Housing Finance Agency house price index, Myrtle Beach, Conway and North Myrtle Beach metro area, from the last quarter of each election year to a year later. The latest year runs to mid-2026. FHFA's margin of error is about 2 percent, so the +3.0% and +0.8% changes may be no change at all.</p></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The record gives no basis for a forecast after this election. The changes swing sevenfold from one election to the next, and we found nothing that ties them to who won. Our <a href="/sell/home-value/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">home value page</a> shows what homes like yours sold for this year.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="plans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Both plans">What do Andrews and Wilson plan for housing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each has seven plans that touch a home or what an owner pays. Each is a proposal. Pick a winner to see them, in their own words where they gave them.</p><div data-ev-tabs><div class="ev-tabs n2" role="tablist" aria-label="Pick a winner"><button type="button" role="tab" id="evp-t0" aria-controls="evp-p0" aria-selected="true" class="p-d">Annie Andrews (D), U.S. Senate</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="evp-t1" aria-controls="evp-p1" aria-selected="false" class="p-r">Alan Wilson (R), governor</button></div><div class="ev-panel" role="tabpanel" id="evp-p0" aria-labelledby="evp-t0"><h3>Annie Andrews' housing plans</h3><div class="ev-grid ev-plans d"><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">1</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">A $50,000 tax credit for first-time buyers</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>a $50,000 tax cut to help first-time homebuyers</q></p><p>Helps with a down payment. It has had no vote. <a href="/buyers/first-time-buyer-tax-credit/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">More</a></p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">2</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">Cut the investor limit from 350 houses to 50</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>We should cut that threshold down several times to 50 homes.</q></p><p>Fewer large companies could keep buying houses.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">3</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">Keep Wall Street off Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac loans</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>ban these Wall Street interests from using Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac mortgages to buy up housing</q></p><p>Large investors would lose those loans for buying homes.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">4</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">Tax cuts for home builders</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>provide tax cuts to homebuilders to incentivize more construction</q></p><p>Aimed at more new homes and more workforce housing. <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">More</a></p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">5</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">End tariffs the president imposes alone</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>end chaotic and unilateral tariffs</q></p><p>She names home building materials, which are part of the cost of a new home.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">6</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">Fix roads and bridges</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>Fix and modernize South Carolina's roads, bridges, and highways</q></p><p>She named no project and no I-73 plan.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">7</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs Congress</span><strong class="t">Homeowners insurance</strong><p>She held a roundtable on rising homeowners insurance. She has no written plan for it.</p></div></div><div class="ev-more"><p class="h">Her other plans, with no direct tie to a home</p><ul><li>A two-year national pause on AI data centers, with no data center costs passed on to power bills.</li><li>Three income tax brackets of 10, 20 and 35 percent, and no tax rise for families under $500,000.</li><li>Health care: reverse federal Medicaid cuts, expand Medicaid here, restore marketplace premium help, and add a public option.</li><li>Drug prices: imports from Canada, a $35 insulin cap for every insured patient, and more prices that Medicare negotiates.</li><li>Families: child care apprenticeships, a larger child tax credit, higher wages and paid family leave.</li><li>Taxes: end breaks for the highest earners and for moving jobs overseas, keep no tax on tips and Social Security, and restore free IRS filing.</li><li>Energy: suspend the federal gas tax and restore clean energy tax cuts.</li><li>Congress: term limits, a balanced budget amendment, a stock trading ban and no pay in shutdowns.</li><li>Other: universal background checks, a path to citizenship with border security, a digital bill of rights, phones out of schools, Roe v. Wade protections, and a vote of Congress before military force.</li></ul></div></div><div class="ev-panel" role="tabpanel" id="evp-p1" aria-labelledby="evp-t1"><h3>Alan Wilson's housing plans</h3><div class="ev-grid ev-plans r"><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">1</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs two-thirds</span><strong class="t">Lower the higher tax on rentals</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>reforming how we assess property taxes on those types of properties</q>, meaning Act 388</p><p>Every rental and second home here pays it. He has published no bill. <a href="#rental-tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Try the slider</a></p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">2</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs the legislature</span><strong class="t">Sign seniors up for the homestead exemption</strong><p>Seniors would get the lower tax on their own home without applying, as reported.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">3</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs the legislature</span><strong class="t">A bigger homestead exemption for disabled veterans</strong><p>A lower tax on a disabled veteran's own home, as reported.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">4</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs the legislature</span><strong class="t">End the car tax for seniors and veterans</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>He will work to eliminate the car property tax for seniors and veterans</q></p><p>Their yearly tax on cars would end.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">5</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs the legislature</span><strong class="t">Faster permits for new homes</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>make it easier for homes to be built, which lowers the cost of those homes</q></p><p>Shorter waits to start building. <a href="/invest/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">More</a></p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">6</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs the legislature</span><strong class="t">Build roads faster</strong><p class="w">In their words: <q>we need to cut down the time it takes to build a road or a bridge</q></p><p>Projects such as I-73. Funding for most of its cost has not been identified.</p></div><div class="ev-card ev-rise"><span class="num">7</span><span class="ev-pill">Needs the legislature</span><strong class="t">Public audits of insurance rate increases</strong><p>Homeowners insurance only. Flood insurance is federal, so it is not part of this plan. <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">More</a></p></div></div><div class="ev-more"><p class="h">His other plans, with no direct tie to a home</p><ul><li>End the state income tax, a long-term goal, and overhaul property taxes, as reported.</li><li>Lower personal property taxes on business equipment, as reported.</li><li>Let the governor pause the state gas tax for up to 120 days, as reported.</li><li>Fully fund public schools and expand school choice.</li><li>Rural clinics and telemedicine.</li><li>Deny state benefits to people here illegally.</li><li>Independent audits of state agencies, led by the lieutenant governor.</li><li>Build the energy grid and add competition, as reported.</li></ul></div></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Neither winner decides impact fees, zoning or where short-term rentals are allowed. County and city councils do.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="675" alt="The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia</a></span></div><figcaption>Horry County's government center in Conway. County Council decides impact fees on new homes and zoning outside the cities.</figcaption></figure><div class="ev-quiz" id="evQuiz"><p class="hd"><span>Three questions</span><b id="evScore"></b></p><div class="ev-q" data-a="no"><p class="q">Can the new governor cut the tax on rentals by himself?</p><div class="o"><button type="button" data-v="yes">Yes</button><button type="button" data-v="no">No</button></div><p class="a"><b>No.</b> It takes two-thirds of each chamber, and Republicans are 4 House votes short.</p></div><div class="ev-q" data-a="no"><p class="q">Can a first-time buyer use a $50,000 federal credit this winter?</p><div class="o"><button type="button" data-v="yes">Yes</button><button type="button" data-v="no">No</button></div><p class="a"><b>No.</b> It is a proposal. It has had no vote in Congress.</p></div><div class="ev-q" data-a="no"><p class="q">Did prices here fall in the year after any election from 2016 to 2024?</p><div class="o"><button type="button" data-v="yes">Yes</button><button type="button" data-v="no">No</button></div><p class="a"><b>No.</b> They rose every time, by 3 to 22 percent.</p></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Sources: Andrews' <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Web-file.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">affordability plan</a> and <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/platform/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">platform</a>; Wilson's <a href="https://wilsonforsc.com/priorities/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">priorities</a>, his agenda as <a href="https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/10/15/republican-nominee-sc-governor-alan-wilson-announces-affordability-agenda/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fox Carolina reported it</a>, and an <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/10/28/full-interview-alan-wilson-shares-priorities-governor-ahead-election-day/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">interview with WIS</a>.</p><script>(function(){var d=document,root=d.getElementById("main");if(!root)return;root.className+=" ev-js";[].forEach.call(d.querySelectorAll("[data-ev-tabs]"),function(g){var bs=g.querySelectorAll("[role=tab]");function show(b){[].forEach.call(bs,function(x){var on=x===b;x.setAttribute("aria-selected",on?"true":"false");x.tabIndex=on?0:-1;var p=d.getElementById(x.getAttribute("aria-controls"));if(p)p.hidden=!on;if(on&&p)[].forEach.call(p.querySelectorAll(".ev-rise"),function(e){e.classList.add("ev-on")})})}[].forEach.call(bs,function(b,i){b.addEventListener("click",function(){show(b)});b.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){var k=e.key==="ArrowRight"?1:e.key==="ArrowLeft"?-1:0;if(k){var n=bs[(i+k+bs.length)%bs.length];n.focus();show(n)}})});show(bs[0])});var s=d.getElementById("evPrice");if(s){var m=254.6,so=109.1,top=1000000*0.06*m/1000,f=function(x){return "$"+(Math.round(x/10)*10).toLocaleString("en-US")};var up=function(){var v=+s.value,a=v*0.06*m/1000,b=v*0.04*m/1000,c=v*0.04*(m-so)/1000;d.getElementById("evV").textContent="$"+v.toLocaleString("en-US");[["evSix",a],["evFour",b],["evHome",c]].forEach(function(p){d.getElementById(p[0]).textContent=f(p[1]);d.getElementById(p[0]+"B").style.width=(p[1]/top*100).