-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="${S.eyebrow}">The short answer</p><h2 style="${S.h2}">Myrtle Beach can have hurricanes</h2><p style="${S.p}">Start with the comparison, because it is the fairest way to frame the risk. Of the five states hurricanes hit most, South Carolina is hit the least. Since 1851 the federal count of direct hurricane hits is 120 for Florida, 64 for Texas, 56 for North Carolina and 54 for Louisiana, against 30 here. The last major hurricane to come ashore on this coast was Hugo, in 1989.</p><p style="${S.p}">We are not going to tell you hurricanes are not a thing here, because they are, and we are not going to tell you they are constant, because the record says otherwise. Since 1989, eight tropical systems have done real damage on the Grand Strand. Most seasons pass without a direct hit. Every damaging storm in that record arrived between August and early October.</p><p style="${S.pLast}">The Atlantic season runs June 1 to November 30. Government climatology puts the peak at September 10, with most activity between mid August and mid October. What follows is each storm and what it did here, from National Weather Service storm reports. You can then judge the risk from facts instead of from a brochure in either direction.</p></div></section>
